Changes from the upstream work
------------------------------

This plugin is not the HarnessMM research codebase. It is a DeepSeek Harness
Host plugin that re-implements the Guarded Decision Reuse (GDR) and Certified
Callback Substitution (CCS) contracts for a general-purpose harness. Relative
to upstream:

  * It is derived from the upstream modules
    `integrations/deepseek_harness/sar_v2.mjs` and
    `integrations/deepseek_harness/cot_jev.mjs` (the `ExecutableSubdag`
    controller and the v6 authored-bank contract), not the full repository.
  * The upstream token-based admission rules (slow-token priors, the
    `net > 1024` threshold, TRAIN-partition medians) are omitted. Admission
    is structural instead: a minimum literal chain length plus a public-byte
    cap plus deterministic receipt-guard satisfaction.
  * The upstream fast-selector path, which posts a one-token classification
    request to a loopback-only provider endpoint and accounts response
    logprobs, is omitted. This plugin never makes an extra model call: a
    request is either answered from an already-decided action or it is not.
  * Provider and model pinning (`options.model !== this.model`) is removed,
    because the harness can change models mid-session.
  * The Python research runners, benchmark controllers, recursive
    self-improvement (RSI) loops, configurations and datasets are not
    included and are not required by this plugin.
  * Persistence, session projection and the compaction-time controller message
    rewrite are not implemented; the authored plan stays in the conversation
    as ordinary assistant text.

Deployment fixes
----------------

The port above passed its own test suite, but those tests validated against
hand-written simplified tool schemas. On a real DeepSeek Harness deployment the
plugin did nothing at all: authored banks were counted, but no action was ever
reused. The following defects were found by live verification and fixed. Each
one has a test that pins it.

  * Guards could never match. The guard vocabulary (return_code / stdout /
    stderr) was defined against the research harness's exec tool, which
    returned a JSON observation. DeepSeek Harness's own tools return rendered
    text: `bash` renders stdout, an optional `[stderr]` section, and a
    trailing `[exit code: N]` marker for a non-zero exit; `read` renders
    line-numbered lines. The guard evaluator now reads that actual rendering.
    The comparison stays an exact string comparison; no guessing was
    introduced, and the paper's contract (a guard is a conjunction of public
    receipt facts) is unchanged.
  * One malformed action bank poisoned the rest of a session. The authoring
    path caught a contract violation but the request path did not, and the
    decision state is re-parsed out of the durable request history. The
    throw escaped the `llm/stream` waterfall, so the model request failed
    instead of falling back to the slow actor.
  * The audit trail overwrote itself: the filesystem service replaces the
    whole file, and its write-intent argument is not a write mode, so an
    append option was ignored. The trail now uses the host's own filesystem,
    because the model-facing filesystem is sandboxed and refuses writes
    outside the session workspace.
  * `harnessmm_receipts` failed exactly when a certificate existed: the
    certificate record carries no timestamp of its own, so the tool result
    held a value that is not losslessly JSON-serializable.
  * The audit trail landed outside the harness home, because the desktop Host
    does not export its home directory to plugins.
  * The plan instruction did not say that a candidate describes an action the
    model has NOT run yet, and that the call being made now is the root. A
    model that planned its own current call had its whole bank rejected. The
    contract was already enforced; the instruction simply did not say so.

Every recorded event now also carries its own timestamp, and `harnessmm_status`
reports the last rejection reasons, so a rising rejection counter says WHICH
contract rule was broken instead of leaving it to guesswork.

No HarnessMM benchmark results, measured token savings, or published numbers
are reproduced here or claimed by this plugin.
